Miles-to-Kilometers Converter — Tkinter Reconstruction Guide
Source: 100 Days of Code — Day 27 (Tkinter, *args, **kwargs)
Note: This source covers a single-window desktop GUI, not a client-server application — there is no database, API, or backend. Sections on DB design, API contracts, and deployment are omitted as not applicable.
🎯 Final Goal
Application / System: Miles to Kilometers Converter (Tkinter GUI)
Objective: Build a desktop GUI application that converts a user-entered mile value into kilometers, demonstrating Tkinter widgets, layout managers, and event-driven callbacks.
Final Expected Behavior: A window titled "Miles to Kilometer Converter" opens, containing a numeric entry field, labels ("Miles", "is equal to", a result value, "Km"), and a "Calculate" button. Typing a number and clicking Calculate updates the result label with the converted kilometer value.
Input: A numeric string typed into the entry widget.
Processing: The string is read, cast to float, multiplied by the conversion constant 1.609, and formatted back into a display string.
Output: The result label's text is updated in place to show the computed kilometer value.
🛠 Technology Stack
Category	Technology	Purpose
Language	Python	Implementation language
GUI Framework	Tkinter (wrapping Tcl/Tk)	Built-in module for creating windows and widgets
Widgets used	Tk, Label, Button, Entry	Window, static text, clickable control, text input
Layout	pack() and grid() geometry managers	Positioning widgets inside the window
Language feature	Default argument values	Used implicitly by Tkinter widget constructors
Language feature	*args (unlimited positional args)	Taught concept; explains variable-length inputs
Language feature	**kwargs (unlimited keyword args)	Explains how Label(**kw) and pack(**kw) accept arbitrary options
IDE	PyCharm (as shown)	Used to write/run the code

[SOURCE] — the project itself uses only Tk, Label, Button, and Entry; other widgets (Text, Spinbox, Scale, Checkbutton, Radiobutton, Listbox) are demonstrated separately in a "widget demo" but are not part of the final converter app, so they're excluded from the required stack per the extraction rule (mentioned-but-unused).
