🧩 Build From Scratch — Quick Reference

1. Create mile_to_kilo_converter.py and import everything from tkinter
2. Create the root window with Tk(), set its title, and place window.mainloop() as the last line of the file
3. Create an Entry widget (miles_input) and four Label widgets (miles_label, is_equal_label, kilometer_result_label starting at "0", kilometer_label)
4. Create a Button widget (calculate_button) with text "Calculate"
5. Lay out all six widgets using .grid(column=, row=) into a 3-column x 3-row layout, never mixing grid with pack
6. Reduce Entry width (e.g. width=7) and add window padding via window.config(padx=20, pady=20)
7. Implement miles_to_km(): read miles_input.get(), cast to float, multiply by 1.609, and set kilometer_result_label.config(text=f"{km}")
8. Set the button's command=miles_to_km (no parentheses) so it fires only on click
9. Run and test: type a mile value, click Calculate, confirm the result label updates with the correct kilometer conversion
Note on scope: Sections covering database design, API contracts, configuration for external services, and deployment from the general reconstruction framework don't apply here — this source is a single-window desktop GUI lesson, not a client-server application. [INFERRED]
